        <section>
            <h3>1. Universal Decomposition Theory</h3>
            <p><strong>Abstract:</strong> The purpose of the present paper is to establish Universal Decomposition Theory (UDT). We begin by defining a <em>decompositional function</em> as an unordered 5-tuple of information concerning a function or function-like object. A decompositional function is not necessarily a function; rather, it is used to measure the failure of an object to be a function and to track information loss, gain, and preservation.</p>
            <p>Next, we define a <em>universe</em> in the context of UDT; they model many theories in ways shown in \(\S 3\) and \(\S 4\). These universes are classified according to types of <em>universe bases</em>, which are collections of information about the elements of the universe and the universe itself. The main results are a series of propositions showing applications of UDT to Mochizuki's IUTT.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h3>2. Topoid Theory</h3>
            <p><strong>Abstract:</strong> In the present paper we develop the theory of topoids. A topoid may be thought of as a generalized space of topological spaces. This paper will be the first in a series of papers aiming for results involving Inter Universal Teichmuller Theory. This paper will not directly have these results, but it will build theory that will hopefully lead to these results.</p>
        </section>
